✅ 0–1 Knapsack Problem — Theory

In 0–1 knapsack:

You have n items

Each item has:

weight wi

profit/value pi

Knapsack has capacity W

You must choose items completely or not at all
(no fractions → unlike fractional knapsack)

Goal: Maximize total profit while total weight ≤ W.

Because decisions are discrete (0 or 1), greedy does not work.

Two classic optimal methods:

Dynamic Programming (DP)

Branch and Bound (B&B)

✅ 1. Dynamic Programming Approach (DP)
✅ Concept

DP solves 0–1 knapsack by building a table DP[i][w]:

i = first i items

w = weight capacity from 0 to W

DP[i][w] = maximum profit using first i items with capacity w

✅ Table Formation Rule

For each item i and capacity w:

If item weight > w → cannot include
→ DP[i][w] = DP[i-1][w]

Otherwise choose max of:

including item i → pi + DP[i−1][w−wi]

excluding item i → DP[i−1][w]

Thus:

DP[i][w] = max( DP[i-1][w],  pi + DP[i-1][w-wi] )


Finally, DP[n][W] gives the answer.

✅ Why DP works?

DP uses optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems:

Best solution for n items depends on best solution for smaller capacity/items.

It avoids recomputation by storing all results.

✅ DP Time & Space Complexity
DP Version	Time Complexity	Space Complexity
Standard DP table	O(nW)	O(nW)
Space-optimized version	O(nW)	O(W)

Where W is capacity, n is number of items.

✅ 2. Branch and Bound Approach
✅ Concept

Branch & Bound treats the knapsack choices as a binary tree:

Each node = decision (include or exclude an item)

Left child = include item

Right child = exclude item

We compute a bound (upper limit on possible profit from that node).
If the bound is less than current best, we prune the branch.

This avoids exploring useless combinations, giving faster performance in many cases.

✅ Upper Bound Calculation

The bound is computed using fractional knapsack idea:

Take remaining capacity and fill using highest profit/weight ratio items.

This gives the maximum possible profit from that node.

Since it may include fractions, bound ≥ true max.

✅ Why Branch & Bound works?

It reduces search by discarding branches that cannot lead to better solutions.

Performs well on average, especially when profits are high or sorted well.

✅ B&B Time & Space Complexity

Worst case (when pruning is poor):

Time: O(2ⁿ)
because exploring entire subset tree is exponential.

Typical / expected case (when pruning is effective):

Much faster than exponential

Often close to polynomial for many input distributions

Space complexity:

O(n) for the recursion/queue and storing node states.

✅ Exam-Ready Summary
✅ Dynamic Programming (DP)

Builds a table storing solutions to subproblems.

Uses recurrence:
DP[i][w] = max( DP[i-1][w], pi + DP[i-1][w-wi] )

Guarantees optimal result.

Time: O(nW)
Space: O(nW) or O(W)

✅ Branch and Bound (B&B)

Uses a tree of decisions (include/exclude).

Uses fractional-knapsack bound to prune branches.

Explores only promising parts of the solution space.

Time: Worst O(2ⁿ), typically much faster
Space: O(n)


